  "query_preview": "..FOR EXTREME SOUTHWESTERN CALIFORNIA INCLUDING ORANGE...\nSAN DIEGO...WESTERN RIVERSIDE AND SOUTHWESTERN SAN BERNARDINO\nCOUNTIES...\n\n...UPDATED AVIATION DISCUSSION...\n\nThis morning...Temperatures are near or a few degrees higher than\nat this time yesterday under clear skies. Offshore flow continues,\nwith the windy locations reporting easterly winds gusting 20-35\nmph at this hour. Several locations reported broken or tied high\ntemperature records. Please see our Record Event Report.\n\nToday will likely be the hottest day of the week as high pressure\naloft and gusty Santa Ana winds combine to produce daytime high \ntemperatures ranging from the mid 90s to low triple digits in the \ncoastal areas and inland valleys. These temperatures are about \n15-25 degrees above normal. Daytime high temperatures remain well \nabove normal west of the mountains through Thursday with moderate \nto high HeatRisk, although slight cooling from the sea breeze will\nincrementally spread farther inland each day as the offshore flow\nweakens. \n\nThe high pressure ridge aloft will weaken a little on Monday and\nconditions become a little less stable. Enough for there to be\nclouds and a slight chance for showers Monday afternoon over the\nSan Bernardino and Riverside County mountains. Any precipitation\nthat occurs will likely be localized and light. The earliest that\nany model guidance shows marine layer clouds/fog returning to the\ncoastal areas is Wed night/Thu morning, but the models have\nhistorically been slow with the return of the marine layer after a\nSanta Ana event so we'll be monitoring this closely.\n\nMost model solutions are now indicating noticeable cooling on \nFriday as the high pressure ridge weakens and gets displaced to\nthe south and east in response to a low pressure trough moving in\nfrom the northwest. Conditions will still be warm west of the\nmountains, with daytime high temperatures 10-15 degrees above\nnormal. The coastal areas will likely see high temps in the 80s\nwhile the inland valleys will still see high temps in the mid to\nupper 90s.\n\nFor next weekend...Significant cooling is likely as a majority of\nensemble members show a low pressure trough deepening along the\nwest coast. Current indications are for temperatures lowering to a\nfew degrees above normal on Saturday then lowering further to a\nfew degrees below normal on Sunday. A slim majority of ensemble\nmembers also show slight chances for widespread light rain on\nSunday but confidence is still low.",
